Never make the same promise twice|The second one is binding|A woman promises her friend she'll never tell; she tells, then promises again, and the second promise is the one the house enforces.|WOMAN; FRIEND; HUSBAND|A kitchen with a back door|She wanted to keep the peace|The second promise is enforced by the house|The kitchen door, the friend's coat, the woman's oath|debt|eerie
Don't promise silence|Silence becomes a person|A man promises to keep silent about what he saw; the silence grows a body and starts following him.|MAN; WIFE; THE SILENCE|A living room with a TV|He was afraid of the consequences|The silence sits in his chair at night|The empty chair, the TV light, the man's mouth|witness|eerie
Never promise a stranger a place to stay|They move in|A woman promises a stranded stranger a bed for one night; the stranger never leaves, and the house starts treating him as family.|WOMAN; STRANGER; HUSBAND|A small flat with one guest room|She felt sorry for him|The stranger has been living there longer than she has|The stranger at the table, the husband's empty chair|invitation|unsettling
