Never forgive a person who hasn't apologized|The apology becomes a visitation|A woman forgives her estranged father without him asking; he starts visiting her in dreams, asking for the forgiveness back.|WOMAN; FATHER; HUSBAND|A bedroom with a phone|She wanted to move on|The father is dead and wants permission to stay|The phone, the father's voice, the woman's wedding photo|invitation|sad
Don't accept an apology from a stranger at night|The stranger is apologizing to someone else|A man accepts an apology from a stranger at night; the stranger says thank you, and the man realizes the apology was meant for the man he is standing in for.|MAN; STRANGER; WIFE|A street corner at night|He didn't want to be rude|The stranger is apologizing to the man's dead brother|The street, the stranger's hand, the brother's photo|witness|sad
Never forgive yourself in a mirror|The mirror doesn't|A man forgives himself in the mirror; the reflection refuses, and starts punishing him from inside the glass.|MAN; REFLECTION; WIFE|A bathroom mirror|He wanted absolution|The reflection is the man he wronged|The mirror, the reflection's hand, the man's face|reflection|eerie
Don't forgive a neighbor's noise|The noise forgives you|A woman forgives her neighbor for the noise; the noise stops, and her own house starts making sounds she can't explain.|WOMAN; NEIGHBOR; HUSBAND|A flat with thin walls|She wanted peace|The noise has moved into her walls|The wall, the silence, the woman's ear|contamination|eerie
Never forgive a dead person|They stay|A son forgives his dead father; the father's ghost stays in the house, refusing to accept the forgiveness.|SON; FATHER; MOTHER|A bedroom with a photo|He wanted closure|The father wants to be blamed|The photo, the father's chair, the son's hand|invitation|sad
Don't forgive a promise|It breaks again|A woman forgives her husband for breaking a promise; he breaks it again, and the second break is worse.|WOMAN; HUSBAND; DAUGHTER|A kitchen with a calendar|She wanted to save the marriage|The promise is the husband's, and he can't keep it|The calendar, the husband's empty chair, the woman's ring|debt|sad
# Debt and obligation
Never owe a favor to a stranger|They collect|A man accepts a favor from a stranger; years later the stranger arrives to collect, and the favor is a life.|MAN; STRANGER; WIFE|A roadside stall|He had no money|The stranger is the man's dead father|The stall, the father's hand, the man's receipt|debt|eerie
Don't accept a gift from someone you can't repay|The gift becomes a debt|A woman accepts a gift from a richer neighbor; the neighbor starts asking for small favors that grow into larger ones.|WOMAN; NEIGHBOR; HUSBAND|A drawing room with a gift|She didn't want to offend|The neighbor is collecting the woman's life piece by piece|The gift, the neighbor's list, the woman's hands|debt|eerie
Never borrow money from a house you're leaving|The house follows|A tenant borrows money from his landlord and moves out; the house appears at his new address, asking for the rent.|TENANT; LANDLORD; WIFE|An empty flat with a suitcase|He was short on cash|The house is the landlord's dead wife|The suitcase, the old house, the new keys|threshold|eerie
Don't lend your umbrella to a stranger|They return it full of rain|A woman lends her umbrella to a stranger; he returns it the next day, and it is still raining inside.|WOMAN; STRANGER; HUSBAND|A shop doorway in the rain|He looked soaked|The umbrella is keeping the stranger's weather|The umbrella, the indoor rain, the woman's floor|contamination|eerie
Never accept a lift from someone you owe|The ride is the payment|A man accepts a lift from a creditor; the ride takes him to a house where his debt is paid in full, and he can't leave.|MAN; CREDITOR; WIFE|A car on a highway|His car had broken down|The house is the creditor's memory|The car, the house, the man's empty wallet|debt|eerie
Don't lend a book to someone who never returns things|The book returns|A man lends a book to a friend who never returns things; the book comes back with the friend's name inside, and the friend doesn't remember borrowing it.|MAN; FRIEND; WIFE|A drawing room with a bookshelf|He wanted to be generous|The friend is in the book|The book, the friend's name, the man's shelf|contamination|eerie
Never owe a neighbor a meal|They come to dinner every night|A woman accepts a meal from a neighbor; the neighbor comes to dinner every night, and the family can't ask her to leave.|WOMAN; NEIGHBOR; HUSBAND|A dining table with five chairs|She was too tired to cook|The neighbor is the previous owner of the house|The table, the neighbor's plate, the family's silence|invitation|unsettling
Don't accept a gift from a dead person|The gift collects|A man accepts a gift from a dead relative's house; the gift starts collecting other objects from the house, and the house starts collecting him.|MAN; WIFE; RELATIVE|A drawing room with a box|He wanted a keepsake|The gift is the dead relative's unfinished business|The box, the house, the man's hands|debt|eerie
Never borrow a child's toy|The child borrows something of yours|A man borrows his nephew's toy; the nephew starts borrowing the man's memories, one by one.|MAN; NEPHEW; SISTER|A child's bedroom with toys|He wanted to play|The toy is the nephew's way into the man's head|The toy, the man's photo album, the nephew's smile|contamination|eerie
Don't owe an apology to someone who died|They wait|A man owes an apology to a friend who died; the friend waits at the man's door every night, not to forgive, but to be apologized to.|MAN; FRIEND; WIFE|A doorstep at night|He was too proud|The friend has been waiting for years|The door, the friend's shadow, the man's hand|debt|sad
